{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794589401840,"sessionID":"ses_e2a23ef3effefsOEWT56TDWh7U","part":{"partID":"prt_1d5dd2121001ThL9AsymUTkm92","sessionID":"ses_e2a23ef3effefsOEWT56TDWh7U","messageID":"msg_1d5dd161a001n6rJpEjefT1qIt","type":"tool","id":"call_fcba8a1d342a4612b602def3","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":".skilled/skills/sk-code/SKILL.md"},"output":"Read file .skilled/skills/sk-code/SKILL.md, lines 1-176\n1: ---\n2: name: sk-code\n3: description: \"Implement, debug and verify code: TypeScript, Python, shell, JSON; quality and review workflow modes with stack surface packets.\"\n4: allowed-tools: [Read, Write, Edit, Bash, Grep, Glob]\n5: version: 3.11.6.0\n6: metadata:\n7:   family: sk-code\n8: ---\n9: \n10: <!-- Keywords: sk-code, code, surface-aware, two-axis, opencode, typescript, python, shell, json, jsonc, github-actions, ci-workflow, sk-code-quality, sk-code-review, sk-code-ci, sk-code-frontend, quality, code-review, verification, debug, implement, workflow-doctrine, mode-registry, workflowmode, packetkind, backendkind, surface-router, evidence-base, surface-packet, guard, adapter, stderr, detector -->\n11: \n12: # Code Family Hub (sk-code)\n13: \n14: One skill has two axes, one shared surface-detection router and one advisor identity.\n15: The **workflow axis** has two code modes that act: sk-code-quality and sk-code-review.\n16: The **surface axis** has three read-only evidence packets the hub bundles alongside a workflow mode: sk-code-ci, sk-code-frontend and sk-code-skilled.\n17: Each packet carries the shared **implement → debug → verify** workflow doctrine (`shared/references/workflow-*.md`, symlinked in) plus its own domain knowledge.\n18: This hub holds NO per-mode logic. It routes by `workflowMode` through `mode-registry.json`, and each mode or surface keeps its own contract in its packet.\n19: \n20: **Version authority.** This file's `version` frontmatter is the hub's release version and matches the newest entry under `changelog/`.\n21: `description.json`, `mode-registry.json` and `hub-router.json` carry the same value. Each hub-root JSON routing file therefore states the same release.\n22: `ROUTER.md` and `README.md` keep their own document versions, as the frontmatter-versioning standard gives every doc but `SKILL.md`.\n23: The authority stops at the hub root. Each `modes[]` packet carries its own version and release cadence.\n24: A packet version is independent of this one by design, so a gap between them is not drift.\n25: \n26: ---\n27: \n28: ## 1. WHEN TO USE\n29: \n30: Use this skill for code-family workflows. Invoke it as `sk-code` with an optional mode hint such as `quality: <request>`. The hub classifies the request, resolves a mode key, and loads the matching nested packet.\n31: \n32: **Workflow axis** (modes that act, one of them chosen as primary):\n33: \n34: | Mode | Use it for | Packet |\n35: |------|------------|--------|\n36: | **sk-code-quality** | Quality gate, P0/P1/P2 author checks, comment hygiene and surface checklists. | `sk-code/sk-code-quality/` |\n37: | **sk-code-review** | Findings-first review, security and correctness baseline, checklists, output contract and PR-state gates. | `sk-code/sk-code-review/` |\n38: \n39: **Surface axis** (read-only, advisor-invisible evidence. When a workflow mode scores, the hub bundles the surface after that primary mode. When no workflow mode scores, the hub routes the surface alone for a lookup, implement, debug or verify task, and the acting agent hands the author gates to `sk-code-quality` before it claims completion):\n40: \n41: | Surface | Carries | Packet |\n42: |---------|---------|--------|\n43: | **sk-code-ci** | GitHub Actions evidence: trigger, filter, permission, timeout, concurrency and caching rules, the protected set, the intentional message-contract exception and the scaffold templates, with the repository-specific values read from a CI profile. Read-only. | `sk-code/sk-code-ci/` |\n44: | **sk-code-frontend** | Stack-agnostic frontend evidence: web markup and CSS, web layout and accessibility, visual values, visual hierarchy and review, motion and interaction, the `tokens.css` starter tokens and Figma-to-code checks. Read-only. | `sk-code/sk-code-frontend/` |\n45: | **sk-code-skilled** | System-code evidence: TypeScript/Python/shell/config standards, hooks, alignment verification, authoring checklists. Bundles the implement → debug → verify workflow doctrine (read-only evidence) for the Skilled surface, and the acting agent applies it. | `sk-code/sk-code-skilled/` |\n46: \n47: **Surface list.** The hub has exactly three surfaces:\n48: - SKILLED (`sk-code-skilled`): the native surface for this framework's own code.\n49: - CI (`sk-code-ci`): the GitHub Actions workflow standards surface.\n50: - Frontend (`sk-code-frontend`): the stack-agnostic web practice and Figma-to-code surface, with visual values, visual hierarchy and review, and motion and interaction guidance, bundled from request vocabulary alone.\n51: \n52: The hub also has two workflow modes: quality (`sk-code-quality`) and review (`sk-code-review`).\n53: Any other sk-code doc that counts surfaces points to this sentence.\n54: \n55: ### When NOT to Use\n56: - Documentation-only changes with no code-work contract - use `sk-doc`.\n57: - Git workflow, branch, commit, PR, merge, or release work - use `sk-git`.\n58: - Pure browser inspection or external MCP transport work - use the relevant MCP skill.\n59: - For a measured Style Reference (design tokens extracted from a live source), use `sk-design-md-generator` first.\n60: - When a request or a frame leaves a visual value open (spacing, type, color, radius, shadow or motion), or a value sits off every token and scale, load `sk-code-frontend` and decide it from its visual-values reference.\n61: - Backend support primitives in `shared/` - they are consumed by the modes, not invoked as a user workflow.\n62: \n63: ---\n64: \n65: ## 2. SMART ROUTING\n66: \n67: Routing is **registry-driven at runtime and authored in `hub-router.json`** in two stages.\n68: Stage 1 (hub → mode) uses `hub-router.json` as the authored mode vocabulary. `mode-registry.json` carries the packet contracts, whose aliases are checked against that vocabulary.\n69: Stage 2 (mode → leaves) uses the root [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md) as the authored leaf-set router.\n70: Both files point back here instead of restating the split.\n71: \n72: The advisor routes every code query to the single identity `sk-code`. Resolve the mode with `node .skilled/bin/compiled-route.cjs --hub sk-code --prompt \"<task>\"` and follow the decision: `route` loads its `targets`, `clarify` and `defer` ask, `reject` refuses.\n73: Compiled routing is the default. The legacy fallback, the kill-switch and the `UNKNOWN_FALLBACK_CHECKLIST` live in the compiled-routing section of [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md).\n74: \n75: A bare implement, debug or verify request with no workflow mode becomes a pure surface bundle, and an unclear code intent asks for disambiguation. Neither path forces a stale default.\n76: Run the compiled-routing directive in [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md) before selecting hub resources.\n77: \n78: ---\n79: \n80: ## 3. HOW IT WORKS\n81: \n82: ### Terms\n83: \n84: - **`workflowMode`**: the public mode or surface key a request resolves to.\n85: - **`packetKind`**: the axis a packet sits on, `workflow` for a mode that acts and `surface` for read-only evidence bundled alongside a mode.\n86: - **`backendKind`**: which backend runs the packet, `surface-router` or `review-cache` for a workflow mode and `evidence-base` for a surface.\n87: - **`surfaceBundle`**: the `routerPolicy.outcomes` entry where one workflow mode leads and zero or more surfaces follow as evidence.\n88: - **`mode-registry.json`**: the registry that owns each mode's discriminator, `surfaceDetection` markers and `advisorRouting`.\n89: - **`hub-router.json`**: stage one's `routerSignals`, `vocabularyClasses` and the surface-bundling policy.\n90: - **`ROUTER.md`**: stage two's `INTENT_SIGNALS` and `RESOURCE_MAP`, plus the `SHARED_CONTROL_RESOURCES` list and the always-loaded `DEFAULT_RESOURCE` preamble.\n91: - **shared control**: a hub-level resource declared in `SHARED_CONTROL_RESOURCES`, which resolves on disk and never projects as a leaf.\n92: - **leaf**: a typed `(workflowMode, leafResourceId)` pair from `leaf-manifest.json` that a `RESOURCE_MAP` path converts to.\n93: - **surface detection**: the shared detection order over the declared surfaces, which tries each specific surface first, then the `SKILLED` fallback, then `UNKNOWN`.\n94: - **`--surface-hint`**: the front-door flag that carries the detected surface into the compiled route, so a hinted surface leads a keyword tie.\n95: - **`UNKNOWN_FALLBACK`**: the load level the router returns, with a disambiguation checklist, when no mode resolves.\n96: \n97: ### Layout\n98: ```\n99: sk-code/\n100:   SKILL.md               # this routing hub (no per-mode code logic)\n101:   README.md              # human-facing front page for the hub\n102:   ROUTER.md              # active stage-two surface router and shared-control declaration\n103:   mode-registry.json     # the two-axis discriminator + advisorRouting contract\n104:   hub-router.json        # lexical routing signals + surfaceBundle policy for hub-local choice\n105:   description.json       # hub advisor descriptor\n106:   graph-metadata.json    # the ONE advisor identity for the whole skill\n107:   leaf-manifest.json     # typed (workflowMode, leaf) pairs the router guards validate\n108:   changelog/             # hub release notes, one v<version>.md per hub release\n109:   feature-catalog/       # current-state inventory of the hub's routing capabilities\n110:   manual-testing-playbook/  # routing and disambiguation scenarios for the hub\n111:   benchmark/             # historical routing benchmark inputs and reports\n112:   sk-code-quality/       # quality mode packet     (workflow)\n113:   sk-code-review/        # review mode packet      (workflow)\n114:   sk-code-ci/            # GitHub Actions workflow standards packet (read-only evidence; rules, protected set and scaffold templates)\n115:   sk-code-frontend/      # stack-agnostic web and Figma-to-code evidence packet (read-only; references/web-visual-values.md, web-visual-hierarchy.md and web-motion-and-interaction.md plus assets/tokens.css)\n116:   sk-code-skilled/      # skilled surface packet (read-only evidence; carries the workflow doctrine)\n117:   shared/                # shared surface-detection router, cross-mode helpers, and the implement/debug/verify workflow doctrine (references/workflow-*.md)\n118: ```\n119: \n120: Each mode or surface packet is self-contained and carries no per-packet `graph-metadata.json`. Only this hub carries one, so the advisor discovers exactly one code skill identity. The implement/debug/verify workflow doctrine is a single shared source under `shared/references/`, and each surface symlinks it in rather than duplicating it.\n121: \n122: ### Backend\n123: The `surface-router` backend is the shared surface-detection router under `shared/`.\n124: It centralizes the detection order over the declared surfaces, the SKILLED fallback and UNKNOWN.\n125: It carries the shared implement → debug → verify workflow doctrine consumed by each surface.\n126: The `review-cache` backend supports the sk-code-review mode's non-mutating review output cache.\n127: The `evidence-base` backend serves the surface packets as read-only domain evidence (`sk-code-ci/`, `sk-code-frontend/`, `sk-code-skilled/`). The hub bundles them alongside the primary mode.\n128: The packet mutates nothing and never carries process. The acting agent applies the doctrine it bundles.\n129: Shared backend material provides surface identity, the workflow doctrine and cross-mode helpers.\n130: It must never gain per-mode workflow contracts.\n131: \n132: The **implement → debug → verify** phases are not standalone modes.\n133: Their surface-agnostic doctrine lives once in `shared/references/workflow-implement.md`, `workflow-debug.md` and `workflow-verify.md`.\n134: Those files are symlinked into each surface, so the active surface carries the full workflow.\n135: A request to implement, debug or verify code causes the router to detect its surface and load that surface's bundled doctrine.\n136: The acting agent applies that doctrine.\n137: \n138: ### Maintenance\n139: \n140: After adding or removing a surface, run `/doctor:sk-code setup`. It checks the hub and names the repair for each failing check.\n141: \n142: ---\n143: \n144: ## 4. RULES\n145: \n146: ### ✅ ALWAYS\n147: - **ALWAYS** resolve a mode through `mode-registry.json`, and never hardcode a router mapping in the hub.\n148: - **ALWAYS** keep mode contracts in the mode packets, so the hub stays routing-only.\n149: - **ALWAYS** keep exactly one `graph-metadata.json` (this hub's) so the advisor sees one skill identity.\n150: - **ALWAYS** give every mode an `advisorRouting` block with `routingClass: \"metadata\"` and the correct `packetSkillName`.\n151: - **ALWAYS** keep the implement/debug/verify workflow doctrine as one shared source under `shared/references/`, symlinked into each surface, and never fork per-surface copies.\n152: - **ALWAYS** keep `ROUTER.md` `RESOURCE_MAP` keys aligned with its intent keys and packet leaves aligned with `leaf-manifest.json`.\n153: - **ALWAYS** keep hub-shared map inputs explicit in `SHARED_CONTROL_RESOURCES`, because they are control resources and never typed leaves.\n154: \n155: ### ⛔ NEVER\n156: - **NEVER** add a `graph-metadata.json` inside a mode packet or `shared/`.\n157: - **NEVER** put per-mode quality, review, or surface workflow logic in the hub.\n158: - **NEVER** hardcode a router mapping outside `mode-registry.json`.\n159: - **NEVER** reintroduce implement, debug, or verify as standalone mode packets, because they are surface-owned doctrine.\n160: \n161: ### ⚠️ ESCALATE IF\n162: - A new code mode is needed - extend `mode-registry.json` and open a packet. Do not bolt logic onto the hub.\n163: - Surface identity and workflow ownership conflict in a way the shared router cannot classify.\n164: \n165: ---\n166: \n167: ## 5. REFERENCES\n168: \n169: - Workflow mode packets: `sk-code-quality/SKILL.md`, `sk-code-review/SKILL.md`.\n170: - Surface evidence packets: `sk-code-ci/SKILL.md`, `sk-code-frontend/SKILL.md`, `sk-code-skilled/SKILL.md`.\n171: - Shared workflow doctrine: `shared/references/workflow-implement.md`, `shared/references/workflow-debug.md`, `shared/references/workflow-verify.md` (symlinked into each surface).\n172: - Registry: `mode-registry.json` (two-axis: `packetKind` discriminates workflow vs surface).\n173: - Hub router signals + surface bundling: `hub-router.json`.\n174: - Surface router and shared controls: `ROUTER.md`.\n175: - Parent-skill pattern: `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/references/parent-skill/parent-skills-nested-packets.md`.\n176: - Sibling example: `.skilled/skills/sk-prompt/`.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file .skilled/skills/sk-code/SKILL.md, lines 1-176\n1: ---\n2: name: sk-code\n3: description: \"Implement, debug and verify code: TypeScript, Python, shell, JSON; quality and review workflow modes with stack surface packets.\"\n4: allowed-tools: [Read, Write, Edit, Bash, Grep, Glob]\n5: version: 3.11.6.0\n6: metadata:\n7:   family: sk-code\n8: ---\n9: \n10: <!-- Keywords: sk-code, code, surface-aware, two-axis, opencode, typescript, python, shell, json, jsonc, github-actions, ci-workflow, sk-code-quality, sk-code-review, sk-code-ci, sk-code-frontend, quality, code-review, verification, debug, implement, workflow-doctrine, mode-registry, workflowmode, packetkind, backendkind, surface-router, evidence-base, surface-packet, guard, adapter, stderr, detector -->\n11: \n12: # Code Family Hub (sk-code)\n13: \n14: One skill has two axes, one shared surface-detection router and one advisor identity.\n15: The **workflow axis** has two code modes that act: sk-code-quality and sk-code-review.\n16: The **surface axis** has three read-only evidence packets the hub bundles alongside a workflow mode: sk-code-ci, sk-code-frontend and sk-code-skilled.\n17: Each packet carries the shared **implement → debug → verify** workflow doctrine (`shared/references/workflow-*.md`, symlinked in) plus its own domain knowledge.\n18: This hub holds NO per-mode logic. It routes by `workflowMode` through `mode-registry.json`, and each mode or surface keeps its own contract in its packet.\n19: \n20: **Version authority.** This file's `version` frontmatter is the hub's release version and matches the newest entry under `changelog/`.\n21: `description.json`, `mode-registry.json` and `hub-router.json` carry the same value. Each hub-root JSON routing file therefore states the same release.\n22: `ROUTER.md` and `README.md` keep their own document versions, as the frontmatter-versioning standard gives every doc but `SKILL.md`.\n23: The authority stops at the hub root. Each `modes[]` packet carries its own version and release cadence.\n24: A packet version is independent of this one by design, so a gap between them is not drift.\n25: \n26: ---\n27: \n28: ## 1. WHEN TO USE\n29: \n30: Use this skill for code-family workflows. Invoke it as `sk-code` with an optional mode hint such as `quality: <request>`. The hub classifies the request, resolves a mode key, and loads the matching nested packet.\n31: \n32: **Workflow axis** (modes that act, one of them chosen as primary):\n33: \n34: | Mode | Use it for | Packet |\n35: |------|------------|--------|\n36: | **sk-code-quality** | Quality gate, P0/P1/P2 author checks, comment hygiene and surface checklists. | `sk-code/sk-code-quality/` |\n37: | **sk-code-review** | Findings-first review, security and correctness baseline, checklists, output contract and PR-state gates. | `sk-code/sk-code-review/` |\n38: \n39: **Surface axis** (read-only, advisor-invisible evidence. When a workflow mode scores, the hub bundles the surface after that primary mode. When no workflow mode scores, the hub routes the surface alone for a lookup, implement, debug or verify task, and the acting agent hands the author gates to `sk-code-quality` before it claims completion):\n40: \n41: | Surface | Carries | Packet |\n42: |---------|---------|--------|\n43: | **sk-code-ci** | GitHub Actions evidence: trigger, filter, permission, timeout, concurrency and caching rules, the protected set, the intentional message-contract exception and the scaffold templates, with the repository-specific values read from a CI profile. Read-only. | `sk-code/sk-code-ci/` |\n44: | **sk-code-frontend** | Stack-agnostic frontend evidence: web markup and CSS, web layout and accessibility, visual values, visual hierarchy and review, motion and interaction, the `tokens.css` starter tokens and Figma-to-code checks. Read-only. | `sk-code/sk-code-frontend/` |\n45: | **sk-code-skilled** | System-code evidence: TypeScript/Python/shell/config standards, hooks, alignment verification, authoring checklists. Bundles the implement → debug → verify workflow doctrine (read-only evidence) for the Skilled surface, and the acting agent applies it. | `sk-code/sk-code-skilled/` |\n46: \n47: **Surface list.** The hub has exactly three surfaces:\n48: - SKILLED (`sk-code-skilled`): the native surface for this framework's own code.\n49: - CI (`sk-code-ci`): the GitHub Actions workflow standards surface.\n50: - Frontend (`sk-code-frontend`): the stack-agnostic web practice and Figma-to-code surface, with visual values, visual hierarchy and review, and motion and interaction guidance, bundled from request vocabulary alone.\n51: \n52: The hub also has two workflow modes: quality (`sk-code-quality`) and review (`sk-code-review`).\n53: Any other sk-code doc that counts surfaces points to this sentence.\n54: \n55: ### When NOT to Use\n56: - Documentation-only changes with no code-work contract - use `sk-doc`.\n57: - Git workflow, branch, commit, PR, merge, or release work - use `sk-git`.\n58: - Pure browser inspection or external MCP transport work - use the relevant MCP skill.\n59: - For a measured Style Reference (design tokens extracted from a live source), use `sk-design-md-generator` first.\n60: - When a request or a frame leaves a visual value open (spacing, type, color, radius, shadow or motion), or a value sits off every token and scale, load `sk-code-frontend` and decide it from its visual-values reference.\n61: - Backend support primitives in `shared/` - they are consumed by the modes, not invoked as a user workflow.\n62: \n63: ---\n64: \n65: ## 2. SMART ROUTING\n66: \n67: Routing is **registry-driven at runtime and authored in `hub-router.json`** in two stages.\n68: Stage 1 (hub → mode) uses `hub-router.json` as the authored mode vocabulary. `mode-registry.json` carries the packet contracts, whose aliases are checked against that vocabulary.\n69: Stage 2 (mode → leaves) uses the root [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md) as the authored leaf-set router.\n70: Both files point back here instead of restating the split.\n71: \n72: The advisor routes every code query to the single identity `sk-code`. Resolve the mode with `node .skilled/bin/compiled-route.cjs --hub sk-code --prompt \"<task>\"` and follow the decision: `route` loads its `targets`, `clarify` and `defer` ask, `reject` refuses.\n73: Compiled routing is the default. The legacy fallback, the kill-switch and the `UNKNOWN_FALLBACK_CHECKLIST` live in the compiled-routing section of [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md).\n74: \n75: A bare implement, debug or verify request with no workflow mode becomes a pure surface bundle, and an unclear code intent asks for disambiguation. Neither path forces a stale default.\n76: Run the compiled-routing directive in [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md) before selecting hub resources.\n77: \n78: ---\n79: \n80: ## 3. HOW IT WORKS\n81: \n82: ### Terms\n83: \n84: - **`workflowMode`**: the public mode or surface key a request resolves to.\n85: - **`packetKind`**: the axis a packet sits on, `workflow` for a mode that acts and `surface` for read-only evidence bundled alongside a mode.\n86: - **`backendKind`**: which backend runs the packet, `surface-router` or `review-cache` for a workflow mode and `evidence-base` for a surface.\n87: - **`surfaceBundle`**: the `routerPolicy.outcomes` entry where one workflow mode leads and zero or more surfaces follow as evidence.\n88: - **`mode-registry.json`**: the registry that owns each mode's discriminator, `surfaceDetection` markers and `advisorRouting`.\n89: - **`hub-router.json`**: stage one's `routerSignals`, `vocabularyClasses` and the surface-bundling policy.\n90: - **`ROUTER.md`**: stage two's `INTENT_SIGNALS` and `RESOURCE_MAP`, plus the `SHARED_CONTROL_RESOURCES` list and the always-loaded `DEFAULT_RESOURCE` preamble.\n91: - **shared control**: a hub-level resource declared in `SHARED_CONTROL_RESOURCES`, which resolves on disk and never projects as a leaf.\n92: - **leaf**: a typed `(workflowMode, leafResourceId)` pair from `leaf-manifest.json` that a `RESOURCE_MAP` path converts to.\n93: - **surface detection**: the shared detection order over the declared surfaces, which tries each specific surface first, then the `SKILLED` fallback, then `UNKNOWN`.\n94: - **`--surface-hint`**: the front-door flag that carries the detected surface into the compiled route, so a hinted surface leads a keyword tie.\n95: - **`UNKNOWN_FALLBACK`**: the load level the router returns, with a disambiguation checklist, when no mode resolves.\n96: \n97: ### Layout\n98: ```\n99: sk-code/\n100:   SKILL.md               # this routing hub (no per-mode code logic)\n101:   README.md              # human-facing front page for the hub\n102:   ROUTER.md              # active stage-two surface router and shared-control declaration\n103:   mode-registry.json     # the two-axis discriminator + advisorRouting contract\n104:   hub-router.json        # lexical routing signals + surfaceBundle policy for hub-local choice\n105:   description.json       # hub advisor descriptor\n106:   graph-metadata.json    # the ONE advisor identity for the whole skill\n107:   leaf-manifest.json     # typed (workflowMode, leaf) pairs the router guards validate\n108:   changelog/             # hub release notes, one v<version>.md per hub release\n109:   feature-catalog/       # current-state inventory of the hub's routing capabilities\n110:   manual-testing-playbook/  # routing and disambiguation scenarios for the hub\n111:   benchmark/             # historical routing benchmark inputs and reports\n112:   sk-code-quality/       # quality mode packet     (workflow)\n113:   sk-code-review/        # review mode packet      (workflow)\n114:   sk-code-ci/            # GitHub Actions workflow standards packet (read-only evidence; rules, protected set and scaffold templates)\n115:   sk-code-frontend/      # stack-agnostic web and Figma-to-code evidence packet (read-only; references/web-visual-values.md, web-visual-hierarchy.md and web-motion-and-interaction.md plus assets/tokens.css)\n116:   sk-code-skilled/      # skilled surface packet (read-only evidence; carries the workflow doctrine)\n117:   shared/                # shared surface-detection router, cross-mode helpers, and the implement/debug/verify workflow doctrine (references/workflow-*.md)\n118: ```\n119: \n120: Each mode or surface packet is self-contained and carries no per-packet `graph-metadata.json`. Only this hub carries one, so the advisor discovers exactly one code skill identity. The implement/debug/verify workflow doctrine is a single shared source under `shared/references/`, and each surface symlinks it in rather than duplicating it.\n121: \n122: ### Backend\n123: The `surface-router` backend is the shared surface-detection router under `shared/`.\n124: It centralizes the detection order over the declared surfaces, the SKILLED fallback and UNKNOWN.\n125: It carries the shared implement → debug → verify workflow doctrine consumed by each surface.\n126: The `review-cache` backend supports the sk-code-review mode's non-mutating review output cache.\n127: The `evidence-base` backend serves the surface packets as read-only domain evidence (`sk-code-ci/`, `sk-code-frontend/`, `sk-code-skilled/`). The hub bundles them alongside the primary mode.\n128: The packet mutates nothing and never carries process. The acting agent applies the doctrine it bundles.\n129: Shared backend material provides surface identity, the workflow doctrine and cross-mode helpers.\n130: It must never gain per-mode workflow contracts.\n131: \n132: The **implement → debug → verify** phases are not standalone modes.\n133: Their surface-agnostic doctrine lives once in `shared/references/workflow-implement.md`, `workflow-debug.md` and `workflow-verify.md`.\n134: Those files are symlinked into each surface, so the active surface carries the full workflow.\n135: A request to implement, debug or verify code causes the router to detect its surface and load that surface's bundled doctrine.\n136: The acting agent applies that doctrine.\n137: \n138: ### Maintenance\n139: \n140: After adding or removing a surface, run `/doctor:sk-code setup`. It checks the hub and names the repair for each failing check.\n141: \n142: ---\n143: \n144: ## 4. RULES\n145: \n146: ### ✅ ALWAYS\n147: - **ALWAYS** resolve a mode through `mode-registry.json`, and never hardcode a router mapping in the hub.\n148: - **ALWAYS** keep mode contracts in the mode packets, so the hub stays routing-only.\n149: - **ALWAYS** keep exactly one `graph-metadata.json` (this hub's) so the advisor sees one skill identity.\n150: - **ALWAYS** give every mode an `advisorRouting` block with `routingClass: \"metadata\"` and the correct `packetSkillName`.\n151: - **ALWAYS** keep the implement/debug/verify workflow doctrine as one shared source under `shared/references/`, symlinked into each surface, and never fork per-surface copies.\n152: - **ALWAYS** keep `ROUTER.md` `RESOURCE_MAP` keys aligned with its intent keys and packet leaves aligned with `leaf-manifest.json`.\n153: - **ALWAYS** keep hub-shared map inputs explicit in `SHARED_CONTROL_RESOURCES`, because they are control resources and never typed leaves.\n154: \n155: ### ⛔ NEVER\n156: - **NEVER** add a `graph-metadata.json` inside a mode packet or `shared/`.\n157: - **NEVER** put per-mode quality, review, or surface workflow logic in the hub.\n158: - **NEVER** hardcode a router mapping outside `mode-registry.json`.\n159: - **NEVER** reintroduce implement, debug, or verify as standalone mode packets, because they are surface-owned doctrine.\n160: \n161: ### ⚠️ ESCALATE IF\n162: - A new code mode is needed - extend `mode-registry.json` and open a packet. Do not bolt logic onto the hub.\n163: - Surface identity and workflow ownership conflict in a way the shared router cannot classify.\n164: \n165: ---\n166: \n167: ## 5. REFERENCES\n168: \n169: - Workflow mode packets: `sk-code-quality/SKILL.md`, `sk-code-review/SKILL.md`.\n170: - Surface evidence packets: `sk-code-ci/SKILL.md`, `sk-code-frontend/SKILL.md`, `sk-code-skilled/SKILL.md`.\n171: - Shared workflow doctrine: `shared/references/workflow-implement.md`, `shared/references/workflow-debug.md`, `shared/references/workflow-verify.md` (symlinked into each surface).\n172: - Registry: `mode-registry.json` (two-axis: `packetKind` discriminates workflow vs surface).\n173: - Hub router signals + surface bundling: `hub-router.json`.\n174: - Surface router and shared controls: `ROUTER.md`.\n175: - Parent-skill pattern: `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/references/parent-skill/parent-skills-nested-packets.md`.\n176: - Sibling example: `.skilled/skills/sk-prompt/`."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\".skilled/skills/sk-code/SKILL.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794589401377,"end":1794589401840}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794589401846,"sessionID":"ses_e2a23ef3effefsOEWT56TDWh7U","part":{"partID":"prt_1d5dd21e1001QekwKBiJBtsoDP","sessionID":"ses_e2a23ef3effefsOEWT56TDWh7U","messageID":"msg_1d5dd161a001n6rJpEjefT1qIt","type":"tool","id":"call_0405dc277f644b13939fb07f","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":".skilled/skills/sk-doc/SKILL.md"},"output":"Read file .skilled/skills/sk-doc/SKILL.md, lines 1-153\n1: ---\n2: name: sk-doc\n3: description: \"Documentation & component authoring hub: skills, agents, commands, READMEs, catalogs, playbooks, changelogs, goals, frontmatter.\"\n4: allowed-tools: [Read, Write, Edit, Bash, Grep, Glob]\n5: version: 2.10.0.0\n6: metadata:\n7:   family: sk-util\n8: ---\n9: \n10: <!-- Keywords: sk-doc, documentation, markdown, authoring, parent-hub, mode-registry, hub-router, workflowmode, packetkind, sk-create-skill, sk-create-readme, sk-create-agent, sk-create-command, sk-create-feature-catalog, sk-create-manual-testing-playbook, sk-create-benchmark, sk-create-changelog, sk-create-diff, sk-create-repo-rule, sk-create-frontmatter, sk-create-goal, sk-create-with-human-voice, sk-create-quality-control, human-voice-rules, hvr, shared-backbone, create-quality-control-pipeline -->\n11: \n12: # Documentation Authoring Hub (sk-doc)\n13: \n14: One advisor identity, fifteen workflow modes across fourteen packets, one shared sk-create-quality-control backbone.\n15: `sk-doc` is the parent hub for documentation and OpenCode-component authoring.\n16: It holds NO per-packet logic: it routes by `workflowMode` through `mode-registry.json`, and each packet keeps its own contract in its nested folder.\n17: The cross-cutting sk-create-quality-control pipeline (validators, global standards, frontmatter/llms/template assets) lives once in `shared/` and is consumed by every packet.\n18: \n19: **Version authority.** This file's `version` frontmatter is the hub's release version and matches the newest entry under `changelog/`; `description.json`, `mode-registry.json` and `hub-router.json` carry the same value, so every hub-root JSON routing file states the same release; `ROUTER.md` keeps its own document version.\n20: \n21: ---\n22: \n23: ## 1. WHEN TO USE\n24: \n25: Use this skill for documentation and OpenCode-component authoring, and for document-quality work. The hub classifies the request, resolves a `workflowMode`, and loads the matching nested packet.\n26: \n27: | Mode | Use it for | Packet | Command |\n28: |------|------------|--------|---------|\n29: | **sk-create-skill** | Scaffold an OpenCode skill (and, via `sk-create-skill-parent`, a parent hub with nested mode packets) | `sk-create-skill/` | `/create:sk-skill`, `/create:skill-parent` |\n30: | **sk-create-readme** | Author a folder README, general or code-folder | `sk-create-readme/` | `/create:readme` |\n31: | **sk-create-agent** | Scaffold an OpenCode agent (permission/authority frontmatter) | `sk-create-agent/` | `/create:agent` |\n32: | **sk-create-command** | Scaffold an OpenCode slash command (argument-hint + allowed-tools + router/presentation split) | `sk-create-command/` | `/create:command` |\n33: | **sk-create-feature-catalog** | Author a feature-catalog inventory package | `sk-create-feature-catalog/` | `/create:feature-catalog` |\n34: | **sk-create-manual-testing-playbook** | Author a manual-testing-playbook package | `sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/` | `/create:manual-testing-playbook` |\n35: | **sk-create-benchmark** | Author MCP-promotion and behavior packages or inputs | `sk-create-benchmark/` | `/create:benchmark` |\n36: | **sk-create-changelog** | Author a global component changelog entry (version bump + topology-aware placement). Packet-local changelogs are deprecated | `sk-create-changelog/` | `/create:changelog` |\n37: | **sk-create-diff** | Produce a local, Git-free before/after review of an edited document (text/Markdown/HTML/DOCX/text-PDF) as a self-contained HTML report | `sk-create-diff/` | `/create:diff` |\n38: | **sk-create-repo-rule** | Create, revise or retire a repo-local rule under `repo-rules/` and wire it into `REPO RULES.md` (four decision tests refuse most requests) | `sk-create-repo-rule/` | `/create:repo-rule` |\n39: | **sk-create-with-human-voice** | Apply the Human Voice Rules to prose, or score prose against them, with a scope gate and a re-scan | `sk-create-with-human-voice/` | `/create:with-human-voice` |\n40: | **sk-create-frontmatter** | Own the YAML frontmatter contract: per-class field rules, the description budget, and the 4-part `version` derivation | `sk-create-frontmatter/` | — (routes via aliases) |\n41: | **sk-create-goal** | Author or revise a packet `goal.md`: the durable directive, three to seven self-contained completion criteria, and the phase-parent binding table (session-goal setting stays with the goal hooks) | `sk-create-goal/` | `/create:goal` |\n42: | **sk-create-quality-control** | Validate / score / optimize an EXISTING document (extract → DQI → HVR → validate) | `sk-create-quality-control/` | (routes via aliases) |\n43: \n44: ### When NOT to Use\n45: - Code implementation, tests, or debugging — use `sk-code`.\n46: - Git worktree, branch, commit, PR, or release work — use `sk-git`.\n47: - Spec-folder workflow / memory / save context — use `system-spec-kit`.\n48: - A measured Style Reference (design tokens extracted from a live source) — use `sk-design-md-generator`.\n49: - The `shared/` backbone is consumed by the packets, not invoked as a user workflow.\n50: \n51: ---\n52: \n53: ## 2. SMART ROUTING\n54: \n55: Routing is **registry-driven at runtime and authored in `hub-router.json`** in two stages.\n56: Stage 1 (hub → mode) selects a mode: `hub-router.json` `vocabularyClasses` is the one authored routing vocabulary.\n57: The `mode-registry.json` aliases, each packet's `Keyword triggers:` line and the root `ROUTER.md` intents are checked against it.\n58: `sk-create-skill/scripts/ci-hub-vocabulary-source.cjs` fails on the `alias`, `stage-two`, `triggers` and `packets` legs; its `stage-two-reverse` leg only reports.\n59: Stage 2 (mode → leaves) lives in the root [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md), which maps the request's authoring intent to the packet-local leaf resources that mode loads.\n60: The two layers stay separate: the hub never emits leaf paths, and the surface router never re-decides the mode.\n61: The advisor routes every documentation and authoring query to the single identity `sk-doc`.\n62: Resolve the mode with `node .skilled/bin/compiled-route.cjs --hub sk-doc --prompt \"<task>\"`.\n63: Act on the decision: `route` loads its `targets`, `clarify` and `defer` ask, `reject` refuses.\n64: Compiled routing is the default.\n65: An unclear documentation intent asks for disambiguation, because `routerPolicy.defaultMode` is `null`.\n66: Run the compiled-routing directive in [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md) before selecting hub resources.\n67: \n68: ---\n69: \n70: ## 3. HOW IT WORKS\n71: \n72: ### Terms\n73: \n74: - **`workflowMode`**: the public packet key a request resolves to, such as `sk-create-skill`.\n75: - **`packetKind`**: `workflow` for every sk-doc packet, because this hub has no surface axis.\n76: - **`backendKind`**: `template-scaffold` for the create-* generators and `sk-create-quality-control` for the quality-control mode.\n77: - **packet**: a nested `sk-create-*` folder that keeps its own authoring contract, references, assets and scripts.\n78: - **`mode-registry.json`**: the registry that owns each packet's discriminator, aliases, command and `advisorRouting`.\n79: - **`hub-router.json`**: stage one's signals, vocabulary classes, default fallback resource and bundle rules.\n80: - **`vocabularyClasses`**: the one authored routing vocabulary that the registry aliases, the `Keyword triggers:` lines and the `ROUTER.md` intents are checked against.\n81: - **`ROUTER.md`**: stage two's `INTENT_SIGNALS` and `RESOURCE_MAP`, which map an authoring intent to the packet-local leaf resources its mode loads.\n82: - **leaf**: a canonical `(workflowMode, leafResourceId)` pair from `leaf-manifest.json` that a `RESOURCE_MAP` path converts to.\n83: - **`defer`**: the outcome that asks for disambiguation, which this hub reaches because `routerPolicy.defaultMode` is `null`.\n84: - **shared backbone**: the `shared/` copy of the sk-create-quality-control pipeline that every packet consumes.\n85: - **`graph-metadata.json`**: the one advisor identity for the whole skill, which no packet or `shared/` directory carries.\n86: \n87: ### Layout\n88: ```\n89: sk-doc/\n90:   SKILL.md               # this routing hub (no per-packet logic)\n91:   ROUTER.md              # stage-two surface router (authoring intent -> leaf sets)\n92:   mode-registry.json     # the fifteen-mode discriminator + advisorRouting (single source of truth)\n93:   hub-router.json        # router signals + vocabulary classes\n94:   description.json       # hub advisor descriptor\n95:   graph-metadata.json    # the ONE advisor identity for the whole skill\n96:   changelog/  manual-testing-playbook/  benchmark/  feature-catalog/\n97:   sk-create-skill/  sk-create-readme/  sk-create-agent/  sk-create-command/\n98:   sk-create-feature-catalog/  sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/\n99:   sk-create-benchmark/  sk-create-changelog/  sk-create-diff/\n100:   sk-create-repo-rule/  sk-create-frontmatter/  sk-create-goal/\n101:   sk-create-with-human-voice/  sk-create-quality-control/    # nested workflow packets\n102:   scripts/               # facade symlinks -> shared/ + owning packets (tool paths only)\n103:   shared/                # sk-create-quality-control backbone: validators, global standards, shared assets\n104: ```\n105: \n106: Each packet is self-contained (its own `SKILL.md`, `README.md`, `changelog/`, and moved `references/`/`assets/`/`scripts/`) and carries **no** `graph-metadata.json`, so the advisor discovers exactly one `sk-doc` identity.\n107: \n108: ### Shared backbone\n109: `shared/` holds the universal sk-create-quality-control pipeline consumed by every packet:\n110: - Generic validator scripts in `shared/scripts/`.\n111: - Cross-cutting standards and vocabulary in `shared/references/`.\n112: - Shared templates in `shared/assets/`.\n113: \n114: The generic validator `shared/scripts/cite-drift-scan.mjs` counts and resolves the `<path>.<ext>:<line>` citations in every tracked skill doc.\n115: It makes zero model calls by default.\n116: `--jev` runs the backend and needs `--out <dir>`, so every call is recorded, and the Jev arm and the advisory live in the sibling `shared/scripts/classifier-cite-drift-scan.mjs`.\n117: `validate_document.py` runs the same check as a non-blocking advisory on the document it validates.\n118: It prints `cite-drift advisory:` lines only when a Jev credential is stored, never changes the exit code, and `JEV_FEATURE_CITE_DRIFT=0` (older name: `SKDOC_CITE_DRIFT_CHECK=0`) skips it.\n119: The `sk-doc/scripts/` root directory keeps facade symlinks pointing inward to `shared/` and the owning packets so tool paths resolve.\n120: There are no hub-root `assets/` or `references/` aggregation directories: consumers reference each packet's own `assets/`/`references/` or the `shared/` backbone directly.\n121: \n122: ---\n123: \n124: ## 4. RULES\n125: \n126: ### ✅ ALWAYS\n127: - **ALWAYS** resolve a packet through `mode-registry.json`; never hardcode a router mapping in the hub.\n128: - **ALWAYS** keep authoring contracts in the packets; the hub stays routing-only.\n129: - **ALWAYS** keep exactly one `graph-metadata.json` (this hub's) so the advisor sees one identity.\n130: - **ALWAYS** keep the root `ROUTER.md` `RESOURCE_MAP` in sync with `leaf-manifest.json` and `leaf-aliases.json` — the leaf sets dual-read to canonical typed pairs at the one contract boundary.\n131: - **ALWAYS** keep the sk-create-quality-control pipeline as one shared source under `shared/`, consumed by the packets.\n132: - **ALWAYS** keep changelogs as real files at the hub and in each packet — never symlinked.\n133: \n134: ### ⛔ NEVER\n135: - **NEVER** add a `graph-metadata.json` inside a packet or `shared/`.\n136: - **NEVER** put per-packet authoring logic in the hub.\n137: - **NEVER** add a surface axis or a `surfaceBundle` outcome — sk-doc is workflow-only.\n138: \n139: ### ⚠️ ESCALATE IF\n140: - A new documentation workflow is needed — extend `mode-registry.json` and open a packet; do not bolt logic onto the hub.\n141: - A packet would become a near-empty shell (generic doctrine, no type-specific behavior) — fold it into `shared/` instead.\n142: \n143: ---\n144: \n145: ## 5. REFERENCES\n146: \n147: - Registry: `mode-registry.json` (fifteen modes across fourteen packets, `packetKind: workflow`).\n148: - Hub router: `hub-router.json` (signals + vocabulary classes).\n149: - Surface router: `ROUTER.md` (authoring intent to packet-local leaf sets).\n150: - Advisor descriptor: `description.json`; skill-graph identity: `graph-metadata.json`.\n151: - Packets: `sk-create-skill/`, `sk-create-readme/`, `sk-create-agent/`, `sk-create-command/`, `sk-create-feature-catalog/`, `sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/`, `sk-create-benchmark/`, `sk-create-changelog/`, `sk-create-diff/`, `sk-create-repo-rule/`, `sk-create-frontmatter/`, `sk-create-goal/`, `sk-create-with-human-voice/`, `sk-create-quality-control/`.\n152: - Shared backbone: `shared/scripts/`, `shared/references/`, `shared/assets/`.\n153: - Parent-skill pattern: `sk-create-skill/references/parent-skill/parent-skills-nested-packets.md`.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file .skilled/skills/sk-doc/SKILL.md, lines 1-153\n1: ---\n2: name: sk-doc\n3: description: \"Documentation & component authoring hub: skills, agents, commands, READMEs, catalogs, playbooks, changelogs, goals, frontmatter.\"\n4: allowed-tools: [Read, Write, Edit, Bash, Grep, Glob]\n5: version: 2.10.0.0\n6: metadata:\n7:   family: sk-util\n8: ---\n9: \n10: <!-- Keywords: sk-doc, documentation, markdown, authoring, parent-hub, mode-registry, hub-router, workflowmode, packetkind, sk-create-skill, sk-create-readme, sk-create-agent, sk-create-command, sk-create-feature-catalog, sk-create-manual-testing-playbook, sk-create-benchmark, sk-create-changelog, sk-create-diff, sk-create-repo-rule, sk-create-frontmatter, sk-create-goal, sk-create-with-human-voice, sk-create-quality-control, human-voice-rules, hvr, shared-backbone, create-quality-control-pipeline -->\n11: \n12: # Documentation Authoring Hub (sk-doc)\n13: \n14: One advisor identity, fifteen workflow modes across fourteen packets, one shared sk-create-quality-control backbone.\n15: `sk-doc` is the parent hub for documentation and OpenCode-component authoring.\n16: It holds NO per-packet logic: it routes by `workflowMode` through `mode-registry.json`, and each packet keeps its own contract in its nested folder.\n17: The cross-cutting sk-create-quality-control pipeline (validators, global standards, frontmatter/llms/template assets) lives once in `shared/` and is consumed by every packet.\n18: \n19: **Version authority.** This file's `version` frontmatter is the hub's release version and matches the newest entry under `changelog/`; `description.json`, `mode-registry.json` and `hub-router.json` carry the same value, so every hub-root JSON routing file states the same release; `ROUTER.md` keeps its own document version.\n20: \n21: ---\n22: \n23: ## 1. WHEN TO USE\n24: \n25: Use this skill for documentation and OpenCode-component authoring, and for document-quality work. The hub classifies the request, resolves a `workflowMode`, and loads the matching nested packet.\n26: \n27: | Mode | Use it for | Packet | Command |\n28: |------|------------|--------|---------|\n29: | **sk-create-skill** | Scaffold an OpenCode skill (and, via `sk-create-skill-parent`, a parent hub with nested mode packets) | `sk-create-skill/` | `/create:sk-skill`, `/create:skill-parent` |\n30: | **sk-create-readme** | Author a folder README, general or code-folder | `sk-create-readme/` | `/create:readme` |\n31: | **sk-create-agent** | Scaffold an OpenCode agent (permission/authority frontmatter) | `sk-create-agent/` | `/create:agent` |\n32: | **sk-create-command** | Scaffold an OpenCode slash command (argument-hint + allowed-tools + router/presentation split) | `sk-create-command/` | `/create:command` |\n33: | **sk-create-feature-catalog** | Author a feature-catalog inventory package | `sk-create-feature-catalog/` | `/create:feature-catalog` |\n34: | **sk-create-manual-testing-playbook** | Author a manual-testing-playbook package | `sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/` | `/create:manual-testing-playbook` |\n35: | **sk-create-benchmark** | Author MCP-promotion and behavior packages or inputs | `sk-create-benchmark/` | `/create:benchmark` |\n36: | **sk-create-changelog** | Author a global component changelog entry (version bump + topology-aware placement). Packet-local changelogs are deprecated | `sk-create-changelog/` | `/create:changelog` |\n37: | **sk-create-diff** | Produce a local, Git-free before/after review of an edited document (text/Markdown/HTML/DOCX/text-PDF) as a self-contained HTML report | `sk-create-diff/` | `/create:diff` |\n38: | **sk-create-repo-rule** | Create, revise or retire a repo-local rule under `repo-rules/` and wire it into `REPO RULES.md` (four decision tests refuse most requests) | `sk-create-repo-rule/` | `/create:repo-rule` |\n39: | **sk-create-with-human-voice** | Apply the Human Voice Rules to prose, or score prose against them, with a scope gate and a re-scan | `sk-create-with-human-voice/` | `/create:with-human-voice` |\n40: | **sk-create-frontmatter** | Own the YAML frontmatter contract: per-class field rules, the description budget, and the 4-part `version` derivation | `sk-create-frontmatter/` | — (routes via aliases) |\n41: | **sk-create-goal** | Author or revise a packet `goal.md`: the durable directive, three to seven self-contained completion criteria, and the phase-parent binding table (session-goal setting stays with the goal hooks) | `sk-create-goal/` | `/create:goal` |\n42: | **sk-create-quality-control** | Validate / score / optimize an EXISTING document (extract → DQI → HVR → validate) | `sk-create-quality-control/` | (routes via aliases) |\n43: \n44: ### When NOT to Use\n45: - Code implementation, tests, or debugging — use `sk-code`.\n46: - Git worktree, branch, commit, PR, or release work — use `sk-git`.\n47: - Spec-folder workflow / memory / save context — use `system-spec-kit`.\n48: - A measured Style Reference (design tokens extracted from a live source) — use `sk-design-md-generator`.\n49: - The `shared/` backbone is consumed by the packets, not invoked as a user workflow.\n50: \n51: ---\n52: \n53: ## 2. SMART ROUTING\n54: \n55: Routing is **registry-driven at runtime and authored in `hub-router.json`** in two stages.\n56: Stage 1 (hub → mode) selects a mode: `hub-router.json` `vocabularyClasses` is the one authored routing vocabulary.\n57: The `mode-registry.json` aliases, each packet's `Keyword triggers:` line and the root `ROUTER.md` intents are checked against it.\n58: `sk-create-skill/scripts/ci-hub-vocabulary-source.cjs` fails on the `alias`, `stage-two`, `triggers` and `packets` legs; its `stage-two-reverse` leg only reports.\n59: Stage 2 (mode → leaves) lives in the root [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md), which maps the request's authoring intent to the packet-local leaf resources that mode loads.\n60: The two layers stay separate: the hub never emits leaf paths, and the surface router never re-decides the mode.\n61: The advisor routes every documentation and authoring query to the single identity `sk-doc`.\n62: Resolve the mode with `node .skilled/bin/compiled-route.cjs --hub sk-doc --prompt \"<task>\"`.\n63: Act on the decision: `route` loads its `targets`, `clarify` and `defer` ask, `reject` refuses.\n64: Compiled routing is the default.\n65: An unclear documentation intent asks for disambiguation, because `routerPolicy.defaultMode` is `null`.\n66: Run the compiled-routing directive in [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md) before selecting hub resources.\n67: \n68: ---\n69: \n70: ## 3. HOW IT WORKS\n71: \n72: ### Terms\n73: \n74: - **`workflowMode`**: the public packet key a request resolves to, such as `sk-create-skill`.\n75: - **`packetKind`**: `workflow` for every sk-doc packet, because this hub has no surface axis.\n76: - **`backendKind`**: `template-scaffold` for the create-* generators and `sk-create-quality-control` for the quality-control mode.\n77: - **packet**: a nested `sk-create-*` folder that keeps its own authoring contract, references, assets and scripts.\n78: - **`mode-registry.json`**: the registry that owns each packet's discriminator, aliases, command and `advisorRouting`.\n79: - **`hub-router.json`**: stage one's signals, vocabulary classes, default fallback resource and bundle rules.\n80: - **`vocabularyClasses`**: the one authored routing vocabulary that the registry aliases, the `Keyword triggers:` lines and the `ROUTER.md` intents are checked against.\n81: - **`ROUTER.md`**: stage two's `INTENT_SIGNALS` and `RESOURCE_MAP`, which map an authoring intent to the packet-local leaf resources its mode loads.\n82: - **leaf**: a canonical `(workflowMode, leafResourceId)` pair from `leaf-manifest.json` that a `RESOURCE_MAP` path converts to.\n83: - **`defer`**: the outcome that asks for disambiguation, which this hub reaches because `routerPolicy.defaultMode` is `null`.\n84: - **shared backbone**: the `shared/` copy of the sk-create-quality-control pipeline that every packet consumes.\n85: - **`graph-metadata.json`**: the one advisor identity for the whole skill, which no packet or `shared/` directory carries.\n86: \n87: ### Layout\n88: ```\n89: sk-doc/\n90:   SKILL.md               # this routing hub (no per-packet logic)\n91:   ROUTER.md              # stage-two surface router (authoring intent -> leaf sets)\n92:   mode-registry.json     # the fifteen-mode discriminator + advisorRouting (single source of truth)\n93:   hub-router.json        # router signals + vocabulary classes\n94:   description.json       # hub advisor descriptor\n95:   graph-metadata.json    # the ONE advisor identity for the whole skill\n96:   changelog/  manual-testing-playbook/  benchmark/  feature-catalog/\n97:   sk-create-skill/  sk-create-readme/  sk-create-agent/  sk-create-command/\n98:   sk-create-feature-catalog/  sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/\n99:   sk-create-benchmark/  sk-create-changelog/  sk-create-diff/\n100:   sk-create-repo-rule/  sk-create-frontmatter/  sk-create-goal/\n101:   sk-create-with-human-voice/  sk-create-quality-control/    # nested workflow packets\n102:   scripts/               # facade symlinks -> shared/ + owning packets (tool paths only)\n103:   shared/                # sk-create-quality-control backbone: validators, global standards, shared assets\n104: ```\n105: \n106: Each packet is self-contained (its own `SKILL.md`, `README.md`, `changelog/`, and moved `references/`/`assets/`/`scripts/`) and carries **no** `graph-metadata.json`, so the advisor discovers exactly one `sk-doc` identity.\n107: \n108: ### Shared backbone\n109: `shared/` holds the universal sk-create-quality-control pipeline consumed by every packet:\n110: - Generic validator scripts in `shared/scripts/`.\n111: - Cross-cutting standards and vocabulary in `shared/references/`.\n112: - Shared templates in `shared/assets/`.\n113: \n114: The generic validator `shared/scripts/cite-drift-scan.mjs` counts and resolves the `<path>.<ext>:<line>` citations in every tracked skill doc.\n115: It makes zero model calls by default.\n116: `--jev` runs the backend and needs `--out <dir>`, so every call is recorded, and the Jev arm and the advisory live in the sibling `shared/scripts/classifier-cite-drift-scan.mjs`.\n117: `validate_document.py` runs the same check as a non-blocking advisory on the document it validates.\n118: It prints `cite-drift advisory:` lines only when a Jev credential is stored, never changes the exit code, and `JEV_FEATURE_CITE_DRIFT=0` (older name: `SKDOC_CITE_DRIFT_CHECK=0`) skips it.\n119: The `sk-doc/scripts/` root directory keeps facade symlinks pointing inward to `shared/` and the owning packets so tool paths resolve.\n120: There are no hub-root `assets/` or `references/` aggregation directories: consumers reference each packet's own `assets/`/`references/` or the `shared/` backbone directly.\n121: \n122: ---\n123: \n124: ## 4. RULES\n125: \n126: ### ✅ ALWAYS\n127: - **ALWAYS** resolve a packet through `mode-registry.json`; never hardcode a router mapping in the hub.\n128: - **ALWAYS** keep authoring contracts in the packets; the hub stays routing-only.\n129: - **ALWAYS** keep exactly one `graph-metadata.json` (this hub's) so the advisor sees one identity.\n130: - **ALWAYS** keep the root `ROUTER.md` `RESOURCE_MAP` in sync with `leaf-manifest.json` and `leaf-aliases.json` — the leaf sets dual-read to canonical typed pairs at the one contract boundary.\n131: - **ALWAYS** keep the sk-create-quality-control pipeline as one shared source under `shared/`, consumed by the packets.\n132: - **ALWAYS** keep changelogs as real files at the hub and in each packet — never symlinked.\n133: \n134: ### ⛔ NEVER\n135: - **NEVER** add a `graph-metadata.json` inside a packet or `shared/`.\n136: - **NEVER** put per-packet authoring logic in the hub.\n137: - **NEVER** add a surface axis or a `surfaceBundle` outcome — sk-doc is workflow-only.\n138: \n139: ### ⚠️ ESCALATE IF\n140: - A new documentation workflow is needed — extend `mode-registry.json` and open a packet; do not bolt logic onto the hub.\n141: - A packet would become a near-empty shell (generic doctrine, no type-specific behavior) — fold it into `shared/` instead.\n142: \n143: ---\n144: \n145: ## 5. REFERENCES\n146: \n147: - Registry: `mode-registry.json` (fifteen modes across fourteen packets, `packetKind: workflow`).\n148: - Hub router: `hub-router.json` (signals + vocabulary classes).\n149: - Surface router: `ROUTER.md` (authoring intent to packet-local leaf sets).\n150: - Advisor descriptor: `description.json`; skill-graph identity: `graph-metadata.json`.\n151: - Packets: `sk-create-skill/`, `sk-create-readme/`, `sk-create-agent/`, `sk-create-command/`, `sk-create-feature-catalog/`, `sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/`, `sk-create-benchmark/`, `sk-create-changelog/`, `sk-create-diff/`, `sk-create-repo-rule/`, `sk-create-frontmatter/`, `sk-create-goal/`, `sk-create-with-human-voice/`, `sk-create-quality-control/`.\n152: - Shared backbone: `shared/scripts/`, `shared/references/`, `shared/assets/`.\n153: - Parent-skill pattern: `sk-create-skill/references/parent-skill/parent-skills-nested-packets.md`."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\".skilled/skills/sk-doc/SKILL.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794589401569,"end":1794589401846}}}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1794589404584,"sessionID":"ses_e2a23ef3effefsOEWT56TDWh7U","part":{"id":"prt_1d5dd2da8001lej3zvPRuNqO6u","sessionID":"ses_e2a23ef3effefsOEWT56TDWh7U","messageID":"msg_1d5dd161a001n6rJpEjefT1qIt","type":"step-finish","reason":"tool-calls","cost":0,"tokens":{"input":1533,"output":90,"reasoning":11,"cache":{"read":44800,"write":0}}}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794589408765,"sessionID":"ses_e2a23ef3effefsOEWT56TDWh7U","part":{"id":"prt_1d5dd3dfd001qFwZpizhfzqkG6","sessionID":"ses_e2a23ef3effefsOEWT56TDWh7U","messageID":"msg_1d5dd2db00014EJpmMAuX7esci","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794589411484,"sessionID":"ses_e2a23ef3effefsOEWT56TDWh7U","part":{"partID":"prt_1d5dd47c0001ATq3ZtIjqUDsTe","sessionID":"ses_e2a23ef3effefsOEWT56TDWh7U","messageID":"msg_1d5dd2db00014EJpmMAuX7esci","type":"tool","id":"call_1037d9983b4047398f23dc2c","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":".skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md"},"output":"Read file .skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md, lines 1-158\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Scope discipline\"\n3: description: \"The requested scope is the deliverable. Adjacent problems get named, not fixed.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"frozen scope\"\n6:   - \"scope lock\"\n7:   - \"scope drift\"\n8:   - \"out of scope\"\n9:   - \"in scope\"\n10:   - \"while I was in there\"\n11:   - \"noticed in passing\"\n12:   - \"adjacent defect\"\n13:   - \"adjacent code cleanup\"\n14:   - \"touch a file outside the ask\"\n15:   - \"rename reformat delete\"\n16:   - \"deviate from the approved plan\"\n17:   - \"amendment over absorption\"\n18:   - \"plan before acting\"\n19:   - \"what needs a yes first\"\n20:   - \"no early stop\"\n21:   - \"natural checkpoint\"\n22:   - \"good stopping point\"\n23:   - \"unrelated change\"\n24: importance_tier: important\n25: contextType: reference\n26: version: 1.0.1.2\n27: ---\n28: \n29: # Rule: Scope discipline\n30: \n31: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before touching anything you were not asked to touch.\n32: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n33: \n34: ## Fires when\n35: \n36: - You notice a defect, a smell, or a stale comment outside the files in scope.\n37: - The fix would be easier if you also changed something else.\n38: - Renaming, moving, reformatting, or deleting beyond the named area.\n39: - Deviating from an approved plan, or substituting your approach for the agreed one.\n40: - Part of the work is blocked and you are deciding what to do with the rest.\n41: \n42: ## The rule\n43: \n44: **The requested scope is the deliverable. Do not narrow it, widen it, or transform it.\n45: Adjacent problems get named, not fixed.**\n46: \n47: ---\n48: \n49: ## 1. THE THREE DRIFTS\n50: \n51: | Drift | What it looks like | Why it costs |\n52: |-------|-------------------|--------------|\n53: | **Narrowing** | Delivering the easy half and framing it as a checkpoint | The operator believes work is done that is not |\n54: | **Widening** | \"While I was in there I also…\" | Unreviewable diffs; a fix and an unrelated regression ship together |\n55: | **Transforming** | Solving the problem you find more interesting than the one asked | The stated problem is still there, and now there is new code too |\n56: \n57: Narrowing is easy to mistake for restraint. Restraint governs *how much you build*, never *how much of the ask you\n58: deliver*.\n59: \n60: ---\n61: \n62: ## 2. WHAT COUNTS AS \"EXPLICITLY IN SCOPE\"\n63: \n64: Law 2 freezes the scope; this section never widens it, it only says how to read it.\n65: Inside the frozen scope these are the *same* change, not adjacent ones:\n66: \n67: - The files named in the request, or the ones it unambiguously implies.\n68: - **Direct callers the change would break.**\n69: - The test covering the behavior you changed.\n70: - Imports, type signatures, and generated files that mechanically follow.\n71: \n72: Anything else is adjacent and goes to §4, including a caller the change does *not* break.\n73: \n74: ---\n75: \n76: ## 3. WHAT ALWAYS NEEDS A YES FIRST\n77: \n78: - Adding a dependency.\n79: - Creating a file outside the area the request named.\n80: - Deleting code you did not write in this task.\n81: - A rename or move that touches other files.\n82: - A formatting, lint, or import-ordering sweep over untouched code.\n83: - Rewriting git history, force-pushing, or touching branches and reflogs.\n84: - Anything in `blast-radius.md`'s irreversible tier.\n85: \n86: ---\n87: \n88: ## 4. THE ADJACENT-DEFECT PROTOCOL\n89: \n90: 1. **Stop.** Do not edit it.\n91: 2. **Record it** in one line: `file:line, what is wrong, why it is out of scope`.\n92: 3. **Finish the in-scope work.**\n93: 4. **Report it in close-out** as a separate list from what you changed.\n94: \n95: Fold it in only if the operator says so, or if leaving it makes the in-scope change\n96: incorrect. Then it was a caller, and §2 covers it.\n97: \n98: ---\n99: \n100: ## 5. DEVIATING FROM AN APPROVED PLAN\n101: \n102: The protocol is `AGENTS.md` §1 PLAN-WORKFLOW LOCK, a hard blocker that outranks this\n103: file. Read it there. What this file adds is the adjacent case, a frozen **scope** you believe is wrong.\n104: \n105: ---\n106: \n107: ## 6. AMENDMENT OVER ABSORPTION\n108: \n109: Do not quietly build what you think was meant. Build what was specified, and in the\n110: same response say what is wrong, what you would change it to, and what it would cost.\n111: The operator decides.\n112: \n113: ---\n114: \n115: ## 7. FINISHING\n116: \n117: `AGENTS.md` §3 Execution Behavior binds: no early stop, no \"natural checkpoint\" on\n118: incomplete work, no asking permission to continue an approved, in-scope step. In scope\n119: terms:\n120: \n121: - **When one part is genuinely blocked, finish every other part in full**, then say\n122:   explicitly what you left out and why. Scaling the work down is the operator's call.\n123: - Proceeding without asking never waives a **mandatory wait:** Gate 3, a\n124:   PLAN-WORKFLOW LOCK approval, the worktree-versus-branch choice, the remote-push\n125:   go-ahead, and every `blast-radius.md` stop-for-yes still block.\n126: \n127: ---\n128: \n129: ## 8. PLAN BEFORE ACTING\n130: \n131: Before the first change on any multi-step work, decide three things and\n132: say them:\n133: \n134: 1. **Which files you will read first.** Reading first is `prevent-overengineering.md` §2's\n135:    pre-write pass. *Which* files is a scope decision, because the set you read is the set\n136:    you will feel entitled to change.\n137: 2. **Which tools or commands will do the work.** Naming them catches a plan that needs\n138:    something outside the frozen scope while it is still cheap to raise.\n139: 3. **How the result will be verified.** `evidence-and-proof.md` §8 owns the proof plan.\n140:    A change with no named check has no defined edge, and an undefined edge is where\n141:    \"while I was in there\" gets in.\n142: \n143: Three sentences is a plan. The failure this prevents is not disorganization, it is the\n144: mid-task realization that the work needs a file nobody scoped, discovered at the moment\n145: when editing it feels easier than asking.\n146: \n147: ---\n148: \n149: ## 9. SELF-CHECK\n150: \n151: - [ ] Every file in my diff is in scope by §2, or has an explicit yes.\n152: - [ ] Adjacent defects are recorded and reported, not fixed.\n153: - [ ] Everything asked for is delivered, or listed as not delivered with a reason.\n154: - [ ] No formatting or cleanup noise rides along with the real change.\n155: - [ ] I named the files, the tools, and the check before the first edit.\n156: - [ ] Nothing entered the plan that was not in the frozen scope.\n157: - [ ] Every action on the always-needs-a-yes list had its yes before it happened.\n158: - [ ] Every deviation from the approved plan was stated and approved, and a plan I judged wrong was followed with the amendment proposed beside it.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file .skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md, lines 1-158\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Scope discipline\"\n3: description: \"The requested scope is the deliverable. Adjacent problems get named, not fixed.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"frozen scope\"\n6:   - \"scope lock\"\n7:   - \"scope drift\"\n8:   - \"out of scope\"\n9:   - \"in scope\"\n10:   - \"while I was in there\"\n11:   - \"noticed in passing\"\n12:   - \"adjacent defect\"\n13:   - \"adjacent code cleanup\"\n14:   - \"touch a file outside the ask\"\n15:   - \"rename reformat delete\"\n16:   - \"deviate from the approved plan\"\n17:   - \"amendment over absorption\"\n18:   - \"plan before acting\"\n19:   - \"what needs a yes first\"\n20:   - \"no early stop\"\n21:   - \"natural checkpoint\"\n22:   - \"good stopping point\"\n23:   - \"unrelated change\"\n24: importance_tier: important\n25: contextType: reference\n26: version: 1.0.1.2\n27: ---\n28: \n29: # Rule: Scope discipline\n30: \n31: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before touching anything you were not asked to touch.\n32: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n33: \n34: ## Fires when\n35: \n36: - You notice a defect, a smell, or a stale comment outside the files in scope.\n37: - The fix would be easier if you also changed something else.\n38: - Renaming, moving, reformatting, or deleting beyond the named area.\n39: - Deviating from an approved plan, or substituting your approach for the agreed one.\n40: - Part of the work is blocked and you are deciding what to do with the rest.\n41: \n42: ## The rule\n43: \n44: **The requested scope is the deliverable. Do not narrow it, widen it, or transform it.\n45: Adjacent problems get named, not fixed.**\n46: \n47: ---\n48: \n49: ## 1. THE THREE DRIFTS\n50: \n51: | Drift | What it looks like | Why it costs |\n52: |-------|-------------------|--------------|\n53: | **Narrowing** | Delivering the easy half and framing it as a checkpoint | The operator believes work is done that is not |\n54: | **Widening** | \"While I was in there I also…\" | Unreviewable diffs; a fix and an unrelated regression ship together |\n55: | **Transforming** | Solving the problem you find more interesting than the one asked | The stated problem is still there, and now there is new code too |\n56: \n57: Narrowing is easy to mistake for restraint. Restraint governs *how much you build*, never *how much of the ask you\n58: deliver*.\n59: \n60: ---\n61: \n62: ## 2. WHAT COUNTS AS \"EXPLICITLY IN SCOPE\"\n63: \n64: Law 2 freezes the scope; this section never widens it, it only says how to read it.\n65: Inside the frozen scope these are the *same* change, not adjacent ones:\n66: \n67: - The files named in the request, or the ones it unambiguously implies.\n68: - **Direct callers the change would break.**\n69: - The test covering the behavior you changed.\n70: - Imports, type signatures, and generated files that mechanically follow.\n71: \n72: Anything else is adjacent and goes to §4, including a caller the change does *not* break.\n73: \n74: ---\n75: \n76: ## 3. WHAT ALWAYS NEEDS A YES FIRST\n77: \n78: - Adding a dependency.\n79: - Creating a file outside the area the request named.\n80: - Deleting code you did not write in this task.\n81: - A rename or move that touches other files.\n82: - A formatting, lint, or import-ordering sweep over untouched code.\n83: - Rewriting git history, force-pushing, or touching branches and reflogs.\n84: - Anything in `blast-radius.md`'s irreversible tier.\n85: \n86: ---\n87: \n88: ## 4. THE ADJACENT-DEFECT PROTOCOL\n89: \n90: 1. **Stop.** Do not edit it.\n91: 2. **Record it** in one line: `file:line, what is wrong, why it is out of scope`.\n92: 3. **Finish the in-scope work.**\n93: 4. **Report it in close-out** as a separate list from what you changed.\n94: \n95: Fold it in only if the operator says so, or if leaving it makes the in-scope change\n96: incorrect. Then it was a caller, and §2 covers it.\n97: \n98: ---\n99: \n100: ## 5. DEVIATING FROM AN APPROVED PLAN\n101: \n102: The protocol is `AGENTS.md` §1 PLAN-WORKFLOW LOCK, a hard blocker that outranks this\n103: file. Read it there. What this file adds is the adjacent case, a frozen **scope** you believe is wrong.\n104: \n105: ---\n106: \n107: ## 6. AMENDMENT OVER ABSORPTION\n108: \n109: Do not quietly build what you think was meant. Build what was specified, and in the\n110: same response say what is wrong, what you would change it to, and what it would cost.\n111: The operator decides.\n112: \n113: ---\n114: \n115: ## 7. FINISHING\n116: \n117: `AGENTS.md` §3 Execution Behavior binds: no early stop, no \"natural checkpoint\" on\n118: incomplete work, no asking permission to continue an approved, in-scope step. In scope\n119: terms:\n120: \n121: - **When one part is genuinely blocked, finish every other part in full**, then say\n122:   explicitly what you left out and why. Scaling the work down is the operator's call.\n123: - Proceeding without asking never waives a **mandatory wait:** Gate 3, a\n124:   PLAN-WORKFLOW LOCK approval, the worktree-versus-branch choice, the remote-push\n125:   go-ahead, and every `blast-radius.md` stop-for-yes still block.\n126: \n127: ---\n128: \n129: ## 8. PLAN BEFORE ACTING\n130: \n131: Before the first change on any multi-step work, decide three things and\n132: say them:\n133: \n134: 1. **Which files you will read first.** Reading first is `prevent-overengineering.md` §2's\n135:    pre-write pass. *Which* files is a scope decision, because the set you read is the set\n136:    you will feel entitled to change.\n137: 2. **Which tools or commands will do the work.** Naming them catches a plan that needs\n138:    something outside the frozen scope while it is still cheap to raise.\n139: 3. **How the result will be verified.** `evidence-and-proof.md` §8 owns the proof plan.\n140:    A change with no named check has no defined edge, and an undefined edge is where\n141:    \"while I was in there\" gets in.\n142: \n143: Three sentences is a plan. The failure this prevents is not disorganization, it is the\n144: mid-task realization that the work needs a file nobody scoped, discovered at the moment\n145: when editing it feels easier than asking.\n146: \n147: ---\n148: \n149: ## 9. SELF-CHECK\n150: \n151: - [ ] Every file in my diff is in scope by §2, or has an explicit yes.\n152: - [ ] Adjacent defects are recorded and reported, not fixed.\n153: - [ ] Everything asked for is delivered, or listed as not delivered with a reason.\n154: - [ ] No formatting or cleanup noise rides along with the real change.\n155: - [ ] I named the files, the tools, and the check before the first edit.\n156: - [ ] Nothing entered the plan that was not in the frozen scope.\n157: - [ ] Every action on the always-needs-a-yes list had its yes before it happened.\n158: - [ ] Every deviation from the approved plan was stated and approved, and a plan I judged wrong was followed with the amendment proposed beside it."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\".skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794589411264,"end":1794589411484}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794589411562,"sessionID":"ses_e2a23ef3effefsOEWT56TDWh7U","part":{"partID":"prt_1d5dd4589001AUzgGItYoA1Ju6","sessionID":"ses_e2a23ef3effefsOEWT56TDWh7U","messageID":"msg_1d5dd2db00014EJpmMAuX7esci","type":"tool","id":"call_2e5097e4961f4cd19d72e3f3","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"rg -n \"MANUAL_PLAYBOOK_RESULT_PERSISTENCE_CONTRACT|renderer-owned|recorded by hand\" --glob '!specs/**' .skilled/skills/sk-doc .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor | head -40"},"output":".skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md:29:<!-- MANUAL_PLAYBOOK_RESULT_PERSISTENCE_CONTRACT -->\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md:30:A scenario run is complete only after its `PASS`, `FAIL`, or `SKIP` outcome and reason are recorded into `system-skill-advisor/benchmark/reports/<dated-run-label>/`. Generated report Markdown is renderer-owned and never hand-authored.\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-quality-control/manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md:19:<!-- MANUAL_PLAYBOOK_RESULT_PERSISTENCE_CONTRACT -->\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-agent/manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md:24:<!-- MANUAL_PLAYBOOK_RESULT_PERSISTENCE_CONTRACT -->\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-agent/manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md:25:A scenario run is complete only after its `PASS`, `FAIL` or `SKIP` outcome and reason are recorded into the skill benchmark reports. Generated report Markdown is renderer-owned.\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-benchmark/manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md:11:<!-- MANUAL_PLAYBOOK_RESULT_PERSISTENCE_CONTRACT -->\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-benchmark/manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md:12:A scenario run is complete only after its `PASS`, `FAIL` or `SKIP` outcome and reason are recorded into the skill benchmark reports folder. Generated report Markdown is renderer-owned.\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-benchmark/manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md:18:The key negative checks are refusal and boundary checks. A correct run leaves an in-flight benchmark in the spec packet. It leaves scoring, runners and renderer-owned reports in their owning lane.\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-benchmark/manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md:66:Run the family-routing scenarios first. They decide which references the later checks should use. Run package-shape scenarios next. Run storage and boundary scenarios last because they depend on the selected family. Keep renderer-owned report checks separate from authored index checks. Record the selected family, the scenario file and the evidence path for every wave.\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-frontmatter/manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md:33:<!-- MANUAL_PLAYBOOK_RESULT_PERSISTENCE_CONTRACT -->\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-frontmatter/manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md:34:A scenario run is complete only after its `PASS`, `FAIL`, or `SKIP` outcome and reason are recorded into `<skill>/benchmark/reports/<dated-run-label>/`. Generated report Markdown is renderer-owned and never hand-authored.\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-readme/manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md:23:<!-- MANUAL_PLAYBOOK_RESULT_PERSISTENCE_CONTRACT -->\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-readme/manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md:24:A scenario run is complete only after its `PASS`, `FAIL` or `SKIP` outcome and reason are recorded into the skill benchmark reports. Generated report Markdown is renderer-owned.\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-feature-catalog/manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md:11:<!-- MANUAL_PLAYBOOK_RESULT_PERSISTENCE_CONTRACT -->\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-feature-catalog/manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md:12:A scenario run is complete only after its `PASS`, `FAIL` or `SKIP` outcome and reason are recorded into the skill benchmark reports folder. Generated report Markdown is renderer-owned.\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-repo-rule/manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md:26:<!-- MANUAL_PLAYBOOK_RESULT_PERSISTENCE_CONTRACT -->\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-repo-rule/manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md:27:A scenario run is complete only after its `PASS`, `FAIL`, or `SKIP` outcome and reason are recorded into `<skill>/benchmark/reports/<dated-run-label>/`; generated report Markdown is renderer-owned and never hand-authored.\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-benchmark/changelog/v1.2.0.0.md:73:`SKILL.md` sections 10 and 11 give in-contract summaries of the two new families, each mirroring section 9: what it is, package and artifact shape, template map, authoring workflow, ALWAYS/NEVER including renderer-owned and lane-local boundaries and success criteria. Section 12 adds a REFERENCES H2 that routes to the packet's family guides and lane-owned normative contracts: the behavior framework, Lane C scoring contract and renderer, plus the Lane B evaluator and reviewer schema.\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-with-human-voice/manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md:25:<!-- MANUAL_PLAYBOOK_RESULT_PERSISTENCE_CONTRACT -->\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-with-human-voice/manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md:26:A scenario run is complete only after its `PASS`, `FAIL` or `SKIP` outcome and reason are recorded into `<skill>/benchmark/reports/<dated-run-label>/`; generated report Markdown is renderer-owned and never hand-authored.\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-goal/manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md:23:<!-- MANUAL_PLAYBOOK_RESULT_PERSISTENCE_CONTRACT -->\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-goal/manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md:24:A scenario run is complete only after its `PASS`, `FAIL` or `SKIP` outcome and reason are recorded into `<skill>/benchmark/reports/<run-label>/` together with the evidence paths. Generated report Markdown is renderer-owned and never hand-authored.\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-changelog/manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md:11:<!-- MANUAL_PLAYBOOK_RESULT_PERSISTENCE_CONTRACT -->\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-changelog/manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md:12:A scenario run is complete only after its `PASS`, `FAIL` or `SKIP` outcome and reason are recorded into the skill benchmark reports folder. Generated report Markdown is renderer-owned.\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/assets/manual-testing-playbook-template.md:182:<!-- MANUAL_PLAYBOOK_RESULT_PERSISTENCE_CONTRACT -->\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/assets/manual-testing-playbook-template.md:183:A scenario run is complete only after its `PASS`, `FAIL`, or `SKIP` outcome and reason are recorded into `<skill>/benchmark/reports/<dated-run-label>/`. Generated report Markdown is renderer-owned and never hand-authored.\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/assets/manual-testing-playbook-snippet-template.md:20:<!-- MANUAL_PLAYBOOK_RESULT_PERSISTENCE_CONTRACT -->\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/assets/manual-testing-playbook-snippet-template.md:21:> **COMPLETION**: This feature run is incomplete until its `PASS`, `FAIL`, or `SKIP` outcome and reason are recorded into `<skill>/benchmark/reports/<dated-run-label>/`. Generated report Markdown is renderer-owned.\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/assets/manual-testing-playbook-conversation-snippet-template.md:19:<!-- MANUAL_PLAYBOOK_RESULT_PERSISTENCE_CONTRACT -->\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/assets/manual-testing-playbook-conversation-snippet-template.md:20:> **COMPLETION**: This feature run is incomplete until its `PASS`, `FAIL` or `SKIP` outcome and reason are recorded into `<skill>/benchmark/reports/<dated-run-label>/`. Generated report Markdown is renderer-owned.\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/SKILL.md:214:<!-- MANUAL_PLAYBOOK_RESULT_PERSISTENCE_CONTRACT -->\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/scripts/validate-playbook-package.cjs:29:const RESULT_PERSISTENCE_MARKER = 'MANUAL_PLAYBOOK_RESULT_PERSISTENCE_CONTRACT';\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md:21:<!-- MANUAL_PLAYBOOK_RESULT_PERSISTENCE_CONTRACT -->\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md:238:The mode should use the retired scenario-persistence wrapper with the scenario ID, variant, verdict, reason, stage and durable outcome evidence. It should not hand-author renderer-owned report Markdown.\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-diff/manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md:13:<!-- MANUAL_PLAYBOOK_RESULT_PERSISTENCE_CONTRACT -->\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook/operator-contract/persist-scenario-result.md:43:- Expected signals: the wrapper exits zero, the run folder contains a persisted PASS outcome, the evidence path is beneath the evidence root and generated Markdown is treated as renderer-owned.\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook/operator-contract/persist-scenario-result.md:57:| MTP-005 | Persist the scenario result | Persist a PASS outcome through the canonical wrapper with durable evidence | `The operator ran MTP-004. Record its PASS result with the evidence path so the run can be audited later.` | 1. `agent: Create one durable evidence file under the controlled evidence root` -> 2. `bash: node the retired scenario-persistence wrapper --skill sk-create-manual-testing-playbook --scenario MTP-004 --variant operator-contract --verdict PASS --reason \"Operator contract validator passed\" --stage operator-contract --evidence evidence/operator-contract.txt` -> 3. `agent: Inspect the generated JSON and report Markdown without editing renderer-owned files` | Step 1: the evidence file is a regular file under the evidence root. Step 2: the wrapper records PASS and exits zero. Step 3: the run folder contains the outcome, reason, evidence path, byte count and hash | The exact prompt, evidence path, wrapper command, wrapper output and exit status and the generated report paths | PASS if the wrapper persists the PASS result and evidence metadata. FAIL if the result is not written, the evidence path is outside the evidence root or report Markdown is hand-edited | 1. Confirm the evidence path resolves beneath the controlled root and is not a symlink. 2. Check the wrapper received the exact scenario ID and verdict. 3. Compare the generated Markdown with the JSON record and report any hand edit |\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook/operator-contract/persist-scenario-result.md:63:3. `agent: Inspect the generated JSON and report Markdown without editing renderer-owned files`\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook/operator-contract/persist-scenario-result.md:67:Step 1 creates the evidence input for the wrapper. Step 2 persists the outcome and records durable evidence metadata. Step 3 reads the generated record and leaves renderer-owned Markdown untouched.\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/scripts/init_skill.py:175:    Runs are now recorded by hand, one row per run folder, so the table columns\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":".skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md:29:<!-- MANUAL_PLAYBOOK_RESULT_PERSISTENCE_CONTRACT -->\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md:30:A scenario run is complete only after its `PASS`, `FAIL`, or `SKIP` outcome and reason are recorded into `system-skill-advisor/benchmark/reports/<dated-run-label>/`. Generated report Markdown is renderer-owned and never hand-authored.\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-quality-control/manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md:19:<!-- MANUAL_PLAYBOOK_RESULT_PERSISTENCE_CONTRACT -->\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-agent/manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md:24:<!-- MANUAL_PLAYBOOK_RESULT_PERSISTENCE_CONTRACT -->\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-agent/manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md:25:A scenario run is complete only after its `PASS`, `FAIL` or `SKIP` outcome and reason are recorded into the skill benchmark reports. Generated report Markdown is renderer-owned.\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-benchmark/manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md:11:<!-- MANUAL_PLAYBOOK_RESULT_PERSISTENCE_CONTRACT -->\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-benchmark/manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md:12:A scenario run is complete only after its `PASS`, `FAIL` or `SKIP` outcome and reason are recorded into the skill benchmark reports folder. Generated report Markdown is renderer-owned.\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-benchmark/manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md:18:The key negative checks are refusal and boundary checks. A correct run leaves an in-flight benchmark in the spec packet. It leaves scoring, runners and renderer-owned reports in their owning lane.\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-benchmark/manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md:66:Run the family-routing scenarios first. They decide which references the later checks should use. Run package-shape scenarios next. Run storage and boundary scenarios last because they depend on the selected family. Keep renderer-owned report checks separate from authored index checks. Record the selected family, the scenario file and the evidence path for every wave.\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-frontmatter/manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md:33:<!-- MANUAL_PLAYBOOK_RESULT_PERSISTENCE_CONTRACT -->\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-frontmatter/manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md:34:A scenario run is complete only after its `PASS`, `FAIL`, or `SKIP` outcome and reason are recorded into `<skill>/benchmark/reports/<dated-run-label>/`. Generated report Markdown is renderer-owned and never hand-authored.\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-readme/manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md:23:<!-- MANUAL_PLAYBOOK_RESULT_PERSISTENCE_CONTRACT -->\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-readme/manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md:24:A scenario run is complete only after its `PASS`, `FAIL` or `SKIP` outcome and reason are recorded into the skill benchmark reports. Generated report Markdown is renderer-owned.\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-feature-catalog/manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md:11:<!-- MANUAL_PLAYBOOK_RESULT_PERSISTENCE_CONTRACT -->\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-feature-catalog/manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md:12:A scenario run is complete only after its `PASS`, `FAIL` or `SKIP` outcome and reason are recorded into the skill benchmark reports folder. Generated report Markdown is renderer-owned.\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-repo-rule/manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md:26:<!-- MANUAL_PLAYBOOK_RESULT_PERSISTENCE_CONTRACT -->\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-repo-rule/manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md:27:A scenario run is complete only after its `PASS`, `FAIL`, or `SKIP` outcome and reason are recorded into `<skill>/benchmark/reports/<dated-run-label>/`; generated report Markdown is renderer-owned and never hand-authored.\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-benchmark/changelog/v1.2.0.0.md:73:`SKILL.md` sections 10 and 11 give in-contract summaries of the two new families, each mirroring section 9: what it is, package and artifact shape, template map, authoring workflow, ALWAYS/NEVER including renderer-owned and lane-local boundaries and success criteria. Section 12 adds a REFERENCES H2 that routes to the packet's family guides and lane-owned normative contracts: the behavior framework, Lane C scoring contract and renderer, plus the Lane B evaluator and reviewer schema.\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-with-human-voice/manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md:25:<!-- MANUAL_PLAYBOOK_RESULT_PERSISTENCE_CONTRACT -->\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-with-human-voice/manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md:26:A scenario run is complete only after its `PASS`, `FAIL` or `SKIP` outcome and reason are recorded into `<skill>/benchmark/reports/<dated-run-label>/`; generated report Markdown is renderer-owned and never hand-authored.\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-goal/manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md:23:<!-- MANUAL_PLAYBOOK_RESULT_PERSISTENCE_CONTRACT -->\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-goal/manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md:24:A scenario run is complete only after its `PASS`, `FAIL` or `SKIP` outcome and reason are recorded into `<skill>/benchmark/reports/<run-label>/` together with the evidence paths. Generated report Markdown is renderer-owned and never hand-authored.\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-changelog/manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md:11:<!-- MANUAL_PLAYBOOK_RESULT_PERSISTENCE_CONTRACT -->\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-changelog/manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md:12:A scenario run is complete only after its `PASS`, `FAIL` or `SKIP` outcome and reason are recorded into the skill benchmark reports folder. Generated report Markdown is renderer-owned.\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/assets/manual-testing-playbook-template.md:182:<!-- MANUAL_PLAYBOOK_RESULT_PERSISTENCE_CONTRACT -->\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/assets/manual-testing-playbook-template.md:183:A scenario run is complete only after its `PASS`, `FAIL`, or `SKIP` outcome and reason are recorded into `<skill>/benchmark/reports/<dated-run-label>/`. Generated report Markdown is renderer-owned and never hand-authored.\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/assets/manual-testing-playbook-snippet-template.md:20:<!-- MANUAL_PLAYBOOK_RESULT_PERSISTENCE_CONTRACT -->\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/assets/manual-testing-playbook-snippet-template.md:21:> **COMPLETION**: This feature run is incomplete until its `PASS`, `FAIL`, or `SKIP` outcome and reason are recorded into `<skill>/benchmark/reports/<dated-run-label>/`. Generated report Markdown is renderer-owned.\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/assets/manual-testing-playbook-conversation-snippet-template.md:19:<!-- MANUAL_PLAYBOOK_RESULT_PERSISTENCE_CONTRACT -->\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/assets/manual-testing-playbook-conversation-snippet-template.md:20:> **COMPLETION**: This feature run is incomplete until its `PASS`, `FAIL` or `SKIP` outcome and reason are recorded into `<skill>/benchmark/reports/<dated-run-label>/`. Generated report Markdown is renderer-owned.\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/SKILL.md:214:<!-- MANUAL_PLAYBOOK_RESULT_PERSISTENCE_CONTRACT -->\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/scripts/validate-playbook-package.cjs:29:const RESULT_PERSISTENCE_MARKER = 'MANUAL_PLAYBOOK_RESULT_PERSISTENCE_CONTRACT';\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md:21:<!-- MANUAL_PLAYBOOK_RESULT_PERSISTENCE_CONTRACT -->\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md:238:The mode should use the retired scenario-persistence wrapper with the scenario ID, variant, verdict, reason, stage and durable outcome evidence. It should not hand-author renderer-owned report Markdown.\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-diff/manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook.md:13:<!-- MANUAL_PLAYBOOK_RESULT_PERSISTENCE_CONTRACT -->\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook/operator-contract/persist-scenario-result.md:43:- Expected signals: the wrapper exits zero, the run folder contains a persisted PASS outcome, the evidence path is beneath the evidence root and generated Markdown is treated as renderer-owned.\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook/operator-contract/persist-scenario-result.md:57:| MTP-005 | Persist the scenario result | Persist a PASS outcome through the canonical wrapper with durable evidence | `The operator ran MTP-004. Record its PASS result with the evidence path so the run can be audited later.` | 1. `agent: Create one durable evidence file under the controlled evidence root` -> 2. `bash: node the retired scenario-persistence wrapper --skill sk-create-manual-testing-playbook --scenario MTP-004 --variant operator-contract --verdict PASS --reason \"Operator contract validator passed\" --stage operator-contract --evidence evidence/operator-contract.txt` -> 3. `agent: Inspect the generated JSON and report Markdown without editing renderer-owned files` | Step 1: the evidence file is a regular file under the evidence root. Step 2: the wrapper records PASS and exits zero. Step 3: the run folder contains the outcome, reason, evidence path, byte count and hash | The exact prompt, evidence path, wrapper command, wrapper output and exit status and the generated report paths | PASS if the wrapper persists the PASS result and evidence metadata. FAIL if the result is not written, the evidence path is outside the evidence root or report Markdown is hand-edited | 1. Confirm the evidence path resolves beneath the controlled root and is not a symlink. 2. Check the wrapper received the exact scenario ID and verdict. 3. Compare the generated Markdown with the JSON record and report any hand edit |\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook/operator-contract/persist-scenario-result.md:63:3. `agent: Inspect the generated JSON and report Markdown without editing renderer-owned files`\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/manual-testing-playbook/operator-contract/persist-scenario-result.md:67:Step 1 creates the evidence input for the wrapper. Step 2 persists the outcome and records durable evidence metadata. Step 3 reads the generated record and leaves renderer-owned Markdown untouched.\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/scripts/init_skill.py:175:    Runs are now recorded by hand, one row per run folder, so the table columns\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\":\"rg -n \\\"MANUAL_PLAYBOOK_RESULT_PERSISTENCE_CONTRACT|renderer-owned|recorded by hand\\\" --glob '!specs/**' .skilled/skills/sk-doc .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor | head -40\"}"},"time":{"start":1794589410697,"end":1794589411562}}}}
